From 3239afc495c723a827d648b5216f03599e1cf824 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Junction 2026 team <junction2026@localhost>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:28:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/13] fix(backend): record depth at half resolution and never
 keep an episode with dropped frames

Lossless 12-bit HEVC of 1280x720 depth costs ~45-65 ms CPU per frame on real
D415 depth (~120 ms on noisy synthetic depth): two cameras at 30 fps next to
the three color encoders did not keep up, and LeRobot's streaming encoder then
dropped depth frames with only a warning, so the depth video ended shorter
than the episode and every later depth frame was misaligned.

- Depth maps are recorded decimated by DEPTH_RECORD_DOWNSCALE (default 2):
  640x360 for 1280x720 color, depth pixel (u, v) = color pixel (2u, 2v),
  every value a measured one. At the full target load (2x 1280x720 color +
  depth, 1080p color, all 30 fps) this encodes in real time with no drops:
  8.4 cores on real D415 depth (10.5 on noisy synthetic), ~100 MB/min per
  depth camera. DEPTH_RECORD_DOWNSCALE=1 keeps full resolution.
- An episode is discarded, with the reason announced to the UI, as soon as a
  video encoder drops a frame or its rows fall more than 0.2 s behind the
  dataset clock (missed ticks compress its time). Saving checks the drops
  again, and after saving every video's length is compared with the rows; a
  mismatch is announced.
- The depth encoder probe encoded one frame: libx265 streams of one or two
  frames fail to mux into mp4 about half of the time (PatchWelcomeError),
  which made depth "unavailable" at random. It now encodes four, and an
  episode needs three frames.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
